course: biology
lesson: 37
title: Insects: butterflies, beetles, flies, mosquitoes, grasshoppers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A green caterpillar hangs upside down, turns into a chrysalis, and inside much of its body melts into a kind of soup that is rebuilt into a butterfly: a body for eating becomes a body for flying.
explain: Butterflies and flies taste with their feet, so a red admiral landing on a rotting plum is tasting it as it lands.
explain: The coloured dust a moth leaves on your fingers is thousands of tiny scales laid like roof tiles, which is why it is kinder to watch than to hold them.
explain: Beetles are the biggest family of animals on Earth: their hard front wings are lids over the flying wings, as you see when a ladybird lifts its spotted cases.
explain: Insects can also be fierce: a bombardier beetle fires a boiling puff with a pop, a swarm of locusts can strip whole farms, and the mosquito biting your arm is, for people, the deadliest animal of all.
fact: Inside a chrysalis much of the caterpillar's body breaks down, and groups of waiting cells build the wings, legs and body of the butterfly.
fact: Butterflies have taste sensors on their feet and drink through a long tube called a proboscis that stays rolled up like a party blower.
fact: Butterfly and moth wings are covered in thousands of tiny scales; some colours, like the blue of the morpho, come from the shape of the scales, not from pigment.
fact: Millions of monarch butterflies fly up to about 4,000 km from Canada and the United States to mountain forests in Mexico for winter, and it takes several generations to fly back north.
fact: Beetles have two hard front wings called elytra that cover the flying wings; about 400,000 kinds have been named, about one in four known kinds of animal.
fact: Dung beetles roll balls of dung to eat or bury, sometimes laying an egg in them, and some steer at night by the glow of the Milky Way.
fact: The bombardier beetle mixes two chemicals in its body to make a spray of about 100 degrees that it shoots from its tail end.
fact: A housefly tastes with its feet, spits juices to turn food to liquid and mops it up; it can carry germs from rubbish and dung, so we cover food, and its eyes see movement very fast, which is why it is so hard to swat.
fact: Only female mosquitoes bite, to get protein for their eggs; the itch is your body reacting to the mosquito's spit, and they find people in the dark by sensing the carbon dioxide gas in their breath, their warmth and their smell.
fact: Some mosquitoes carry malaria, which kills about 600,000 people a year, mostly young children in Africa, so mosquitoes are the deadliest animals to people.
fact: Grasshoppers sing by rubbing a hind leg against a wing and crickets by rubbing their wings together; crickets have their ears on their front legs.
fact: Desert locusts are grasshoppers that, when crowded, change colour and behaviour and form swarms of billions that eat crops.
fact: The glow-worm is a beetle: the wingless female makes a cold green light in her tail with a chemical reaction, which wastes almost no energy as heat, to show flying males where she is.
exercise: What happens inside a chrysalis? | Much of the caterpillar breaks down and is rebuilt into a butterfly | The caterpillar sleeps and grows wings on its back | The caterpillar lays a special egg inside it that hatches into a butterfly | A butterfly moves in from outside
exercise: How does a red admiral taste a rotting plum? | With taste sensors on its feet | With its wings | With its antennae only | It cannot taste anything, it only smells
exercise: What does a butterfly drink through? | A long rolled-up tube called a proboscis | Small sharp teeth | Its feet | Small holes in the edges of its wings
exercise: What is the coloured dust on your fingers after touching a moth's wing? | Thousands of tiny scales from the wing | Pollen from flowers | Poison on the wing to keep the birds away from it | The moth's dried sweat
exercise: Where do monarch butterflies spend the winter? | In mountain forests in Mexico | In caves in Canada | Under the snow in Alaska | In warm houses in New York, behind curtains
exercise: Does one monarch butterfly fly to Mexico and back again? | No, it takes several generations to fly back north | Yes, every single one makes the round trip twice in its life | Yes, they fly back the next week | No, monarchs never return north
exercise: What does a ladybird lift before it flies? | Its hard front wing cases, the elytra | Its six legs, to tuck them in | Its head, so it can look up at the sky and the wind | Its spots, which come off
exercise: About what share of all known kinds of animal are beetles? | About one in four | About one in a hundred | About one in every two | About one in a thousand
exercise: Why does a dung beetle roll a ball of dung? | To eat it or bury it, sometimes with an egg inside | To build a house out of it | To play football with other beetles in the field all day | To clean the field for farmers
exercise: How do some dung beetles steer at night? | By the glow of the Milky Way | By the smell of the moon on the grass | By following the cows across the field | By the sound of owls hooting nearby
exercise: What happens when a bombardier beetle is attacked? | It sprays a hot chemical puff from its tail with a pop | It plays dead for an hour | It bites the attacker with its sharp poisoned jaws | It rolls into a ball
exercise: How does a housefly eat cake? | It spits juices to make it liquid and mops it up | It bites off small pieces with its teeth and chews them | It sucks it like a mosquito | It stores it in its wings
exercise: Why do we cover food when flies are around? | Flies may carry germs from rubbish and dung | Flies eat up all the food on the table in a minute or two | Flies make the food go cold | Flies lay poison on food
exercise: Which mosquitoes bite people? | Only the females, to get protein for eggs | Only the males | All mosquitoes, both males and females, whenever they can | Only baby mosquitoes
exercise: Why does a mosquito bite itch? | Your body reacts to the mosquito's spit | The mosquito leaves a tiny hair in your skin | The mosquito always bites you twice in one go | The bite gets sunburnt when you go outside
exercise: How does a grasshopper chirp in long grass? | By rubbing a hind leg against a wing | By blowing air out of its mouth like a whistle | By clicking its jaws | By tapping its feet on stems
exercise: Why do desert locusts form swarms? | Crowding after rains changes their colour and behaviour | They are chased by birds | They follow a queen locust | Strong winds blow them all together into one place
exercise: Why does a glow-worm shine on a June night? | The female shows flying males where she is | To scare away hedgehogs | To see where she is walking in the dark grass at night | Because she ate a firefly
test: In a cartoon a caterpillar just grows wings while still being a caterpillar. What really happens? | It forms a chrysalis, where its body is rebuilt into a butterfly | It eats wings off leaves | It borrows wings from a moth | It turns into a bird
test: my cousin says the monarchs in Mexico remember the trees from last year. right? | No, the ones arriving have never been there before | Yes, they come back every year | Yes, their mothers come with them and show them the way to the trees | No, monarchs never go to Mexico
test: What would happen if you rubbed all the scales off a butterfly's wing? | It would lose its colours and patterns | It would grow a full set of new scales within a minute or two | It would fly faster | Nothing, the scales are just dirt
test: The blue morpho of South America looks bright blue. Where does its blue come from? | The shape of its scales bouncing light | Blue colour it gets from eating blue berries | Blue blood showing through its thin wings | The blue sky reflecting off its shiny body
test: A mosquito keeps finding you in the dark tent. How? | It senses the carbon dioxide in your breath, your warmth and smell | It sees you with night vision | It hears your heartbeat | It follows the light from your phone or your torch across the tent
test: Which animal kills the most people each year? | The mosquito, because some carry malaria | The shark | The lion | The crocodile, because it hides in rivers
test: shut up, flies dont have taste, they just land on stuff. who's right? | Flies taste with their feet as they land | Flies taste with their wings | Flies have no taste at all | Flies taste only with their big eyes as they land on things
test: Why is it so hard to swat a fly? | Its eyes see movement very fast | It can hear your thoughts before you move | It has extra eyes on its feet and wings | It can turn invisible for a moment
test: In a game, a glow-worm is a wiggly worm that lights up. What would you correct? | A glow-worm is a beetle, and the glowing one is the wingless female | It is a snake that glows | It is a worm that lights up with electricity | Only male glow-worms glow
test: Why does the glow-worm's light not get hot like a bulb? | Light from a chemical reaction wastes almost no energy as heat | It is far too small to get hot like a bulb | It keeps the light in ice | It only glows for a second
test: What would happen if a swarm of desert locusts landed on a farm? | It could eat the crops in a very short time | It would pollinate the crops and make them grow better | It would help the crops grow | It would only eat the weeds
test: A cricket's song is coming from the grass. Where are its ears? | On its front legs, below the knees | On top of its head | It has no ears at all | On its wings
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process within the chrysalis? | Much of the larval body is broken down and reconstructed into the adult form | The caterpillar merely sleeps until wings emerge | The caterpillar is replaced by a different animal | The caterpillar lays an egg which, in due course, develops into the winged butterfly
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the mosquito is accounted the deadliest animal to humankind. | Certain species transmit malaria | Its bite is directly and highly venomous | It attacks in exceedingly large swarms | It contaminates the drinking water supplies
exam: Ascertain which sex of mosquito feeds upon blood. | The female exclusively | The male exclusively | Both sexes, equally | Neither sex
exam: It is given that a ladybird prepares for flight. Identify the structures it raises. | The hardened forewings, or elytra | The hind legs and the feet | The antennae | The abdomen
